const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Clinical Research Protocol and Proposal Writing","serviceType":"Protocol writing, ethics submission documents and funder proposals","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/research-proposal/","description":"Clinical research protocols to SPIRIT 2025 and ICH E6(R3), with the whole ethics submission set assembled around them — information sheet, consent.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What should be included in a clinical research protocol?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For a trial, the 34 items of SPIRIT 2025 plus its timeline figure: administrative information with the registration details and the version, the background and rationale, the objectives, the design named explicitly, the setting and eligibility criteria as they will actually be applied, the interventions with their dose, route, schedule and discontinuation criteria, the outcomes with their instruments and time points, the sample size with its assumptions, recruitment, allocation and concealment, blinding, data collection and management, the statistical methods, monitoring and harms reporting, and the ethics and dissemination plans. The two items applicants most often omit are the schedule of enrolment, interventions and assessments — which SPIRIT asks for as a figure and which is what makes the visit burden legible to a committee at a glance — and the stopping rules. For observational studies there is no SPIRIT, which is why they are so often under-specified; write the protocol against the STROBE items instead, because that forces the decisions that otherwise get made during analysis."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I justify my sample size?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By stating every assumption and where it came from, not by producing a number. A committee or a reviewer cannot disagree with 212; they can disagree with a 0.45 standard deviation effect taken from two named trials in a different population, and that is a conversation rather than a query letter. So: the smallest difference worth detecting, argued as a clinical judgement with its source; the variance and where it came from; the significance level and whether the test is one- or two-sided, with a defence if it is one-sided; the power; the allowance for attrition, justified from your own site’s figures rather than from convention. Then the feasibility check, which is the part that is usually missing: if the inflated target is 250 over eighteen months at a clinic seeing 41 eligible patients a month, say so and state the consent rate you are assuming. A committee reads an unchecked recruitment target as a risk to participants, because a trial that cannot recruit exposes people to research that cannot answer its question. Our free sample size calculator does the arithmetic; what the protocol adds is the argument."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why did my ethics submission come back?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Most submissions get a query letter, including good ones, and a committee that asks nothing is unusual rather than reassuring. But the queries cluster, and four causes account for most of them. The participant information sheet is written above the reading level of the population — this is the single commonest, and it is a real ethical point rather than a stylistic one, because a sheet nobody can read has obtained a signature rather than consent. Two documents disagree, usually because the design changed and only one was updated. The sample size is a number without a justification. And something administrative is missing or stale: an unsigned CV, an unnumbered protocol version, an insurance confirmation that has not arrived. Three of those four are entirely preventable by a second reader going through the set against the committee’s own checklist before submission, which takes a day and saves a meeting cycle."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will you write my informed consent form?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We will draft the document to your instruction and to your committee’s format, and we will measure its readability against a stated target rather than asserting that it is in plain language. What we will not do, and what no writing service can do, is take the responsibility. The investigator and the sponsor hold the duty for the ethics submission, and the consent process — the conversation with the participant, the judgement about capacity, the decision that this person has genuinely understood what they are agreeing to — is a clinical act. It belongs to the clinician taking consent and it cannot be bought. We say this plainly because consent forms and risk-benefit analyses are routinely drafted without anybody stating who owns them, and that ambiguity is a real exposure for a client rather than a convenience. Where a document is drafted by us, the covering application says so, exactly as an acknowledgement in a paper would."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between a research proposal and a protocol?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A protocol is the operating document: what will be done, to whom, when, by whom, and what happens when something goes wrong. Its readers are an ethics committee, a regulator and, later, the study team itself. A proposal is a competitive document: why this question matters, why this team, why now, and what it will cost. Its readers are a funding panel scoring against published criteria. They share a methods section and differ in almost everything else — the proposal compresses the background into a page that establishes a gap rather than demonstrating reading, adds a timeline, a budget and a team case, and is written to a page limit that is enforced. Most clinical studies need both, and writing one from the other is a rewrite rather than a reformat. The commonest mistake is submitting a protocol to a funder: it reads as thorough and uncompetitive, because it never says why anybody should care."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I align a proposal with a funding agency's requirements?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Read the call and the scoring criteria before writing anything, and write to the criteria in the order and with the weight the funder gives them. Horizon Europe scores Excellence, Impact, and Quality and Efficiency of the Implementation, in that order, and most clinical proposals lose marks on impact because they write a hope where a pathway is asked for. NIH reads the Specific Aims page first and reads everything after it in the light of that page, so it is written first and rewritten last; the data management and sharing plan has been required since the 2023 policy and is still routinely thin. NIHR wants patient and public involvement evidenced rather than asserted — who was involved, when, and what changed as a result — alongside a plain English summary that is genuinely plain. ICMR, DBT and DST prescribe their formats and their budget heads, and the forms fail more proposals than the science does. And every funder has page limits that are enforced by software rather than by goodwill."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When do I have to register the trial?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Before the first participant is enrolled, and this is one of the few things in research that genuinely cannot be fixed later. The ICMJE requires prospective registration in a WHO primary registry or on ClinicalTrials.gov as a condition of consideration at member journals, and the registration date is public and permanent — so a trial registered after enrolment starts carries that fact forever, and it will be the first thing an editor sees years later when you submit the paper. Which registry depends on where you are: the Clinical Trials Registry – India, ClinicalTrials.gov in the United States, ISRCTN or ClinicalTrials.gov in the UK alongside Health Research Authority approval, and the EU Clinical Trials Information System under Regulation 536/2014 for trials running in member states. Register the outcomes you actually intend, in the order you intend them, because the registered record is what your eventual paper will be checked against for outcome switching."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is ICH-GCP still E6(R2)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. ICH E6(R3) is current: its Principles and Annex 1 were adopted by ICH and by the CHMP on 23 July 2025 and are described as a replacement for E6(R2). Annex 2, which covers pragmatic and decentralised trial designs, reached Step 4 in June 2026 and applies in the EU from 15 January 2027, with a consolidated version of all three parts published in July 2026. A protocol written in 2026 that still cites E6(R2) as the current standard is a year out of date, and it is a visible error to anybody in regulatory affairs. The practical difference is proportionality: R3 asks for quality management built around the risks that actually matter to participant safety and to the reliability of the results, rather than uniform procedure applied regardless of risk. For investigator-initiated studies that is usually good news, because it makes a monitoring plan proportionate to a low-risk study defensible rather than something to apologise for."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you guarantee ethics approval or a grant award?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and we will not quote a success rate as though we could. Ethics approval is a committee’s judgement and a funding decision is a panel’s, and both are made by people who have not met us. What we can commit to is the thing that is actually in our control: a submission set that is complete, internally consistent, written to the committee’s own format and to the current versions of the frameworks that govern it, read by a second person against the committee’s checklist before it goes in. That turns administrative queries into substantive ones, and a substantive query is usually answerable by a chair’s action rather than another full meeting. We will also tell you when we think a design cannot answer its question, which occasionally ends an engagement before it starts. That is a better outcome than a well-written protocol for a study that should not run."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Physician writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Research proposal","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/research-proposal/"}]}];

